ome. But you'd be arriving at it because it fits your actual circumstances, not because it fits a rule that was built for different circumstances than the ones you're in now. The strongest version of your father's argument is really "this is a time-tested arrangement, don't discard it without good reason." That's worth taking seriously. But "good reason" here can just be: your wife makes more money, or she wants to return to her career, or your job doesn't offer paternity flexibility hers does. Those are legitimate reasons that would justify deviating from the pattern, and they don't require you to prove your father's family did something wrong—just that your situation is different enough to warrant a different call.
