h some pre-installed gender trait. If nurturing were purely biological and fixed, you wouldn't see it develop in fathers who become primary caregivers, but you do, consistently. So here's the honest version: pregnancy and birth are biological and female-specific, full stop. But "who works and who stays home for the next several years" isn't determined by strength or birth—it's determined by income, career trajectories, personal preference, and who's actually good at what. Your dad's conclusion doesn't follow from his premise even when the premise is true.
