22:53:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A few problems here, starting with the biology itself. **It's not actually true across species** This isn't even accurate as a description of the animal kingdom. Look at actual examples: - In many bird species, both parents share nest duties and feeding, sometimes with the male doing the bulk of incubation (emperor penguins, some sandpipers). - Among wolves and many canids, both parents hunt and provision the pack; other pack members help raise young too. - Female lions do almost all the hunting in a pride, while males primarily defend territory—the reverse of what you're describing. - In many primate species closer to humans, paternal care is substantial—marmoset and tamarin fathers carry infants most of the time. - Seahorses: males carry and give birth to the young. "Males hunt, females nurse" isn't a universal law of nature—it's one pattern among many, and animals show enormous variation depending on species-specific pressures. So the premise you're building on isn't solid to begin with. **Even if it were universal, that wouldn't tell us what humans should do** This is the deeper issue, separate from the biology. Nature does all kinds of things we don't treat as models for human behavior. Male lions kill cubs that aren't their own when they take over a pride. Many species abandon offspring entirely. Chimps engage in violent territorial raids. Nobody argues humans should do these things just because they occur elsewhere in nature. "It happens in nature" isn't the same as "it's good" or "we should do it too." This is a well-known reasoning error—the naturalistic fallacy, drawing an "ought" from an "is." Nature doesn't optimize for human wellbeing or fairness or happiness; it optimizes for reproductive success, sometimes through pretty brutal means. We don't use it as a moral guide anywhere else, so it's inconsistent to invoke it selectively here. **Humans aren't just animals responding to instinct** The whole reason this conversation is even happening—you and your wife weighing incomes, career trajectories, personal preferences, talking it through—is itself evidence that humans don't just default to fixed instinctual roles the way, say, spawning salmon do. We build institutions, we negotiate, we make individual choices based on circumstances. That capacity is part of human nature too, arguably the most distinctive part. So even setting aside that the animal comparison doesn't hold up factually, "nature does X" was never going to settle what you and your wife should do. The actual answer is still going to come from the practical stuff: income, career paths, who wants what, what fits your family—not from lion prides or wolf packs.
