sing children in stable homes, honesty, loyalty). Those survive the test because the underlying need never went away. Other traditions were solutions to specific, contingent economic or technological constraints (who works based on physical strength, land-based marriage alliances, rigid inheritance-by-eldest-son rules). Those often don't survive the test once the constraint disappears, because there's nothing left holding them up except habit. **Where "who works after parental leave" falls** This is much closer to the second category. It was solved by physical strength and reproductive biology because those were binding constraints on who could do what job and who had to recover from birth. Those constraints have loosened dramatically for most jobs and most of the post-recovery period. That's why I've been treating it as open to reassessment, while I wouldn't apply the same argument to say "marriage should be discarded" or "committed co-parenting doesn't matter"—those rest on needs that haven't changed at all. You're not on a slippery slope toward nihilism about all tradition. You're just being asked to check, tradition by tradition, whether the "why" still applies—and for this specific question, I don't think it does, even though for lots of other things around family life, it clearly still does.
