ally taking it seriously enough to ask what was generating it, rather than treating the pattern itself as the thing to be explained by pattern alone ("it happens a lot, therefore it should keep happening"). So: real phenomenon, real cause, and a cause that doesn't apply to your household the way it applied to your grandfather's. You can hold both — "this pattern is real and makes complete sense historically" and "our specific decision isn't well answered by it" — without one canceling the other out. That's the most honest answer I can give you, and it's not a dodge: the historical pattern is real and explicable. It's just explicable by things that don't constrain you and your wife in the same way anymore.
